ch as pathology review, staging refinement, and consideration of adjuvant treatments. [Data: Entities (4956, 16); Relationships (10928)]\\n\\nSeveral supporting concepts reinforce what surgery is intended to accomplish in cancer care: it is framed as an “operation or procedure” used to remove cancer from the body, and it is closely tied to whether a tumor is considered resectable (i.e., removable completely by surgery). These relationships reflect how feasibility (resectability) is foundational to choosing surgery as a main modality. [Data: Entities (16, 8341, 989, 1650, 2586); Relationships (8341, 1499, 2733, 4725)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Radioactive iodine (RAI) therapy is a targeted post-surgical modality that relies on iodine uptake by thyroid cells\",\n      \"explanation\": \"RADIOACTIVE IODINE (RAI) THERAPY is described as a post-thyroidectomy treatment that selectively destroys thyroid tissue and thyroid cancer cells that absorb iodine, aiming to reduce recurrence risk and treat known or suspected spread while minimizing exposure to most healthy tissues. This positions RAI as a targeted systemic/whole-body-capable therapy that is mechanistically linked to thyroid-cell iodine avidity. [Data: Entities (5039, 5058, 5060); Relationships (11116, 11118,+more)]\\n\\nThe community further specifies that RAI therapy uses IODINE-131, administered orally, and that RAI can be used to kill remaining cancer cells after total thyroidectomy. Preparatory and safety elements are also represented: a low-iodine diet may be required beforehand, and radioactivity can exit via body fluids, requiring temporary precautions. [Data: Entities (5040, 5041, 5045, 5103); Relationships (11103, 11107, 11111, 11248,+more)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Thyroid cancer subtype distinctions matter for therapy selection, including limits of radiation and RAI effectiveness\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The dataset distinguishes PAPILLARY THYROID CANCER and FOLLICULAR THYROID CANCER as subtypes for which radiation therapy is described as rarely used. This indicates that, within this community, external radiation is not emphasized as a routine modality for these common differentiated subtypes. [Data: Entities (5070, 4974); Relationships (11153, 11154)]\\n\\nMEDULLARY THYROID CANCER is explicitly characterized as not being effectively treated with RAI therapy, and it is also described as inheritable and able to run in families. Together, these points highlight clinically relevant divergence: while RAI is central for iodine-avid disease, it is not uniformly applicable across thyroid cancer types. [Data: Entities (5007, 4956); Relationships (10995)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Clinical (pre-treatment) stage and pathologic staging are differentiated, with surgery enabling the most definitive staging\",\n      \"explanation\": \"CLINICAL (PRE-TREATMENT) STAGE is framed as a best-guess estimate derived from testing before surgery, with the explicit caveat that surgery is needed to know exactly how much cancer is in the body. This emphasizes the provisional nature of pre-operative staging in contrast to post-operative findings. [Data: Entities (2043, 16); Relationships (3410)]\\n\\nPATHOLOGIC STAGING and PATHOLOGIC STAGE (pTNM) are tied directly to examining REMOVED TISSUE obtained during surgery, making surgery essential for definitive pTNM designation. The relationships further note that pathologic stage is then used to determine treatment options after surgery, connecting diagnostic confirmation to downstream care planning. [Data: Entities (2569, 3917, 599, 16); Relationships (2736, 8182, 4728)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Follow-up monitoring and endocrine management link TSH testing to thyroid hormone dose adjustment decisions\",\n      \"explanation\": \"A TSH BLOOD TEST is represented as a lab test used by the doctor in evaluating suspected thyroid nodules and guiding next steps, while DOSE ADJUSTMENT is described